WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:06.279
Welcome back! So, in the last part,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:10.420
I showed a way to solve Towers of Hanoi just by counting up in binary,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.773
a solution that I learned from computer scientist Keith Schwarz.

00:00:13.773 --> 00:00:18.120
If somehow you landed here without watching that you probably want to go check it out

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:19.570
Here, in this video,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.860
I want to describe a constrained variant of that puzzle

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:26.600
and how it relates to finding a curve that fills Sierpinski's triangle.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.360
The problem variant is that ... you can think these discs are in a line.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.180
You're only allowed to

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:36.520
move a disk from one spindle to an adjacent one.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:41.340
And, so, now the question is, solve Towers of Hanoi

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.500
So, for example, in the previous one,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:44.420
we start by moving

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.690
disc 0 to B, and the next move was to move disc 1 over to C, but you can't do that because you can get

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:51.000
1 straight over to C.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:51.690
Exactly.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.940
So, it's more complicated.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:58.360
But the idea for this modified version is still similar.

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:00.370
So if I want to move disc 3 -- let's say

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.360
I'm trying to get it from A to C.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:10.020
So, as before, I can't move disc 3 unless this tower above it, 2, 1, and 0, are out of the way.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:18.100
So, ultimately, I'd like to get this tower to be not blocking it, but there's two ways it can block it now

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.460
So, previously it just had to not be on top.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.920
Now it has to not only not be on top; it can't be adjacent, either.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.580
So, really, what I want to do is take this tower of 0, 1, and 2

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:31.340
and somehow -- I don't know -- get it to thing C

00:01:31.909 --> 00:01:36.099
then move disc B over, then get it off of C, back to A

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:41.180
Then move disc 3 from spindle B to spindle C,

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.460
and then move that tower all the way back.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.600
The smaller case breaks down essentially the same way.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.000
You solve it for two discs, move disc number 2,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.160
solve for 2 again,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.080
move disc number 2,

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.360
then solve for two discs, yet again.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:07.760
As you keep subdividing in this self-similar pattern, expanding each subproblem into its own set of subproblems,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:15.940
eventually, you get to the smallest subproblem of them all -- moving a tower of size 1, which just involves moving disc number 0 twice.

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:27.960
As with the unconstrained case, this is going to give you the most efficient solution,

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.300
since at every scale you're only doing what you have to do

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:38.029
you have to move that subtower with 0, 1, and 2 over to peg C if you plan on moving disc number 3.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.469
And you have to move it back to A in order to move disc 3 again

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.700
And you have to move it all the way back to C a third time.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.880
There's just never any room for inefficiency once you break it down in terms of subproblems like this.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.230
And just as the unconstrained puzzle mirrored the pattern of counting in binary

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:04.260
The breakdown of this constrained problem is mirrored by counting in base 3, also known as ternary.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:09.060
Here, let's take a moment and talk about what base 3 feels like -- what the rhythm of counting there is.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:14.460
So, as you know, our usual counting system, base 10, has ten separate digits,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:20.680
and binary, or base 2, has two separate digits, so ternary is a system of representing numbers

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:25.360
where you only have three distinct symbols available -- 0, 1, and 2.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.400
I don't even know what you call them.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.520
So, it's not a bit; it's not a digit; is it at trit?

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.320
I think we were talking about this earlier. It just doesn't sound right.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:37.280
They are indeed called trits, but, hey, if that sounds a little bit off to you,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.900
just ask a French speaker how they feel about our convention of calling binary digits bits.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:47.360
Anyway, back to what we were doing, let's think about the rhythm of counting in ternary

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:51.980
You start by just counting through the trits: 0, 1, 2.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.640
Then, you have to roll over to a second trit in the threes place.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:01.151
Then, you count to 4, which is 11; 5, which is 12;

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:06.060
and you have to roll over again, to 20, representing six.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.000
Then, 21, 22, at which point you have to roll over twice, to the nines place,

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.740
writing the number nine as 100.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.860
Just like base 2 and base 10, there's a certain self-similarity to this pattern.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.120
At all scales, it looks like counting up to some amount, rolling over,

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.890
counting to that same amount,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.880
rolling over again,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.460
then counting to that same amount a third time.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.140
But this is the pattern that we just saw with the constrained Towers of Hanoi:

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:45.420
do a subtask, make some kind of larger movement, do a subtask, make that same larger movement, then do the subtask a third time.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:53.700
So, just like binary counting mirrors the solution to the unconstrained Towers of Hanoi,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:59.200
counting in ternary is going to mirror the recursive pattern for solving the constrained hours of Hanoi.

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:05.280
This gives a really nice and methodical way to solve the constrained problem, just by counting.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:13.100
Every time you're only changing that last trip, move disc number 0. So for the first two moves when you're counting, 1, 2,

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.360
you'll be moving disc 0.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:22.660
Whenever you roll over just once to the threes place, such as when you count from two up to three, represented by 10,

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:24.600
you move disc number 1.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:33.780
And continuing on like this, you'd flip the last; move 0; flip the last; move 0; roll over, writing 20; and then move disc 1.

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.080
then, twice more, you flip the last and move disc 0.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:43.600
Then, whenever you roll over twice to the nines place, you move disk number 2.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.960
Again, it's pretty fun to just sit back and watch this play out.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.220
It takes a while, though. For four discs, you're going to have to count all the way up to 2222,

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:59.300
which is ternary for 3⁴ - 1, which is 80.

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.900
That means that solving this involves 80 steps.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:08.040
Nevertheless, like I said before, this is the most efficient solution.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:14.880
But this also then gives you something very cool; you can ask, how many different configurations are there

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.880
for Towers of Hanoi?

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:20.440
Actually, let's take a moment and think about this. How many total

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:27.420
configurations of discs on pegs are possible, where the disks on a given peg have to be in descending order of size?

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.760
The answer is 3⁴,

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.780
81 possible states that your puzzle can be in.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.800
To see this, notice that there are three choices for where to put that biggest disc,

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:49.480
then three choices for where to put the next-biggest disc,

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.860
and for each successive disc, you have three choices for where to place it.

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:04.140
And remember, this process of solving by counting in ternary

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:12.280
involves taking 3⁴ - 1 steps, which means from start to end, you're going to hit 81 different configurations.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:17.360
And if you think about it, it can't hit a given configuration twice.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:24.220
If it did, there would be some more efficient solution out there -- by working up to the first time where you hit that configuration,

00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:30.160
then skipping over everything in between that and the second time you hit that same configuration, then just continuing.

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:33.600
I have, in a sense, sort of a grand tour. This will not only

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:38.340
solve Towers of Hanoi; this will go through every single possible configuration of the disks.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:39.480
Pretty cool, right?

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:43.760
Well, here's where things get awesome. For those of you familiar with graphs,

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.080
there is a very beautiful structure to these configurations.

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:53.000
What I'm going to do is represent each one of these configurations with a dot, a node in our graph.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:03.180
Then, we say that two different configurations are connected if you could move from one to the other

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.480
with some legal Towers of Hanoi move.

00:08:06.020 --> 00:08:08.360
And this time, I mean an unconstrained move,

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:13.800
so configurations are still considered connected even if it requires a move from peg A to peg C.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:17.560
When you go through and connect all of the configurations like this,

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:20.460
finding all the edges that represent some kind of move,

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:25.040
the graph structure for Towers of Hanoi has this suspiciously familiar shape.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:29.320
Now, let's zoom in on it, and think about where this pattern is coming from.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:35.040
For example, this node here representing the start state, with all the discs on peg A,

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.420
is going to be connected to this one, which is the result of moving disc 0 over to peg B.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:46.420
And both of those are connected to this one, which is the result of moving disc 0 to peg C.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.140
These three configurations make up a little triangle in our graph.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.120
That is, the three configurations are all mutually connected to each other.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:01.600
In fact, any configuration is going to be part of one of these triangles somewhere in the graph. You just consider

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.060
what happens as you freely move around disc number 0 among the three pegs.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:17.360
Now, to understand how these little triangular islands are interconnected, take a look at these two nodes.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:24.320
They differ only by a movement of disc number 1 from peg A to peg C, so they're going to be connected by an edge.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:28.720
This edge is kind of a bridge between two different triangle islands.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:36.220
In fact, those two triangles, along with this other one, form kind of a meta-triangle when you consider all the movements of disc 1.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:43.260
Each little clump of three nodes here represents a different position for disc number 1, and the Triforce pattern as a whole

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:48.320
represents all configurations you can get by moving around discs number 1 or 0.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:59.860
And self-similarly, a movement of disc number 2 gives you a bridge from one of these Triforce patterns to a new one.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.700
And the three possible places where disc 2 can live

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:08.240
give us these three separate Triforce patterns, which are each connected by a little bridge.

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:14.440
The fact that there are only two nodes in one of these Triforce patterns that has an edge going out of the pattern itself

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:21.260
is just a reflection of the fact that it's hard to move disc number 2. That only happens in those very special configurations

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.020
where disc 1 and disc 0 are both out of the way.

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:35.680
As you consider more and more discs, this pattern continues. The graph structure for Towers of Hanoi configurations

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:39.200
is one big Sierpinski triangle. Isn't that crazy?

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:45.380
So now let's think about what it means for this method of solving the constrained problem

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:49.700
by counting in ternary to walk through all possible configurations.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:57.440
What it's going to give us is a way to wander through this graph and hit each node once and only once.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.780
This is crazy to me. If you just look at the Sierpinski graph structure,

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:07.880
it's not clear at first that such a path is even possible, and yet we found one just by counting.

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.260
If that's not beautiful, I don't know what is.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:25.680
For the final animation here, I want to show you guys what those paths look like for Sierpinski graphs of higher and higher order.

