00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:45.600
All right, so, for this last animation here,

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:48.980
I'm going to show the path that walks through the Sierpinksi graph,

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:54.060
according to the way in which ternary counting solves the constrained Towers of Hanoi puzzle.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:13:00.220
If I fade out that graph just to emphasize the path, here's what it looks like for higher and higher orders,

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:02.820
corresponding to Towers of Hanoi with more and more discs.

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:09.720
What you're seeing is very similar to the idea of space-filling curves that I talked about in the Hilbert curve video.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:14.160
It's just that the limit here fills a fractal instead of the square.

00:13:14.740 --> 00:13:22.380
Isn't that crazy? You can have a curve that fills Sierpinski's triangle, which I totally don't think of as a curve-type thing.

